    {
      "filename": "18 June 1815 – Victory at Waterloo – The liveliest joy !.jpg",
      "title": "File:18 June 1815 – Victory at Waterloo – The liveliest joy !.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/18_June_1815_%E2%80%93_Victory_at_Waterloo_%E2%80%93_The_liveliest_joy_%21.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:18_June_1815_%E2%80%93_Victory_at_Waterloo_%E2%80%93_The_liveliest_joy_!.jpg",
      "width": 3262,
      "height": 2123,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Alta Falisa",
      "description": "18 June 1815, late evening. \nAnd suddenly broke out news of victorious fights at hardly known places the day before, like : La Haye Sainte, Belle-Alliance, Goumont (Hougoumont)..., – as the daily L'Oracle enthusiastically reported from Brussels : \"Partout la haine contre l'usurpateur a éclaté de la manière la plus énergique ; hier, au moment de la bataille, la crainte glaçait toutes les âmes ; aujourd'hui la joie la plus vive est peinte sur toutes les figures. (...)\"  –  (L'Oracle, 19 June 1815.) \nTRANSLATION – Everywhere hatred against the usurper has burst forth in the most energetic manner; yesterday, at the time of the battle, fear froze all souls; today the liveliest joy is painted on all faces. (...) \n\n\nMont-Saint-Jean Museum : ►(File:18 June 1815 – Waterloo – Mont-Saint-Jean, Museum.jpg).",
      "dateCreated": "2021-02-24",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "hougoumont-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u150",
      "mediumPath": "_m/u/1/5/0/hougoumont-wp/18-june-1815-victory-at-waterl-medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/u/1/5/0/hougoumont-wp/18-june-1815-victory-at-waterl-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "18 June 1815 – Waterloo – Hougoumont. To the Victims of the napoléonic régime.jpg",
      "title": "File:18 June 1815 – Waterloo – Hougoumont. To the Victims of the napoléonic régime.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0c/18_June_1815_%E2%80%93_Waterloo_%E2%80%93_Hougoumont._To_the_Victims_of_the_napol%C3%A9onic_r%C3%A9gime.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:18_June_1815_%E2%80%93_Waterloo_%E2%80%93_Hougoumont._To_the_Victims_of_the_napol%C3%A9onic_r%C3%A9gime.jpg",
      "width": 3840,
      "height": 2386,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Alta Falisa",
      "description": "18 June 1815 – Hougoumont Farm, at Braine-l'Alleud. –– Silent memorial to Victims of the napoléonic régime. \nFrom left to right : behind the trees, the various corps de logis adjoining each others ; futher right, the Stables and the Great Barn ; in front of the Stables, the chapel.  The Ferraris Map (1770-1778), folio 78, depicts the same buildings under the name Chateau d'Hougoumont, also commanding a vast formal garden to its west.  All peaceful and thriving – at that time.\n\nAftermath of Napoleone Buonaparte's incursion on June 18th, 1815 : \nThis place, the villa of a gentleman, was reduced to ruins, resembling a house burnt down.  A little chapel, too, belonging to it has also been injured. (Charles Campbell, The Traveller's Complete Guide Through Belgium &amp; Holland, 2nd edition, 1817, 69.\n\n\nIn the words of a poet : \nAt Hougoumont the hottest strife had been, \nWhere trees and walls the mournfoul records bore \nOf war's wild rage, trunks pierc'd with many a wound, \nAnd roofs half burnt and rafters on the ground. \n(Robert Southey, Poet's Pilgrimage to Waterloo, 1816, Part III : The Field of Battle, stanza XL).\n\n\n.",
      "dateCreated": "2016-04-10 09:50:37",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "battle-of-waterloo-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u150"
    },
    {
      "filename": "18 June 1815 – Waterloo – Hougoumont Farm. To the Second Battalion.jpg",
      "title": "File:18 June 1815 – Waterloo – Hougoumont Farm. To the Second Battalion.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b0/18_June_1815_%E2%80%93_Waterloo_%E2%80%93_Hougoumont_Farm._To_the_Second_Battalion.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:18_June_1815_%E2%80%93_Waterloo_%E2%80%93_Hougoumont_Farm._To_the_Second_Battalion.jpg",
      "width": 2250,
      "height": 2858,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Alta Falisa",
      "description": "18 June 1815 – Waterloo – Hougoumont Farm. To the Second Battalion of Coldstream Guards. \nTablet on the outer walls of Hougoumont Farm, on the right of the northern gate whose shutting to the enemy was such a crucial step towards the Final Victory. \n\nIn memory of the Officers and Men of the 2nd Battalion Who Died Defending This Farm  \nJune 18th 1815\n\nAffixed by the 3rd Regiment Foot Guards for the  Waterloo 200 commemorations, June 2015. \n\nThe motto Nemo me impune lacessit translates as \" No one provokes me with impunity\".",
      "dateCreated": "2016-04-10 10:13:25",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "hougoumont-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "u150",
      "mediumPath": "_m/u/1/5/0/hougoumont-wp/18-june-1815-waterloo-hougoumo-1-medium.webp",
